8f68bd34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=9$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ce0c9ed-8f49-5909-b44d-93bd60aa3e7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409432 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=5$\n- $n=8$\n- $n=9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c433443-9a9c-5d0b-84b5-bc247b100915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409433 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a4a4ae5-eba9-597a-b12c-2ce503fbc86a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409434 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $17≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $23≡-10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42227076-6750-5c0c-9261-152604f91699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409435 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $8≡23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=4$\n- $n=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80a88f89-ac3c-581b-83f4-0bc0a136d398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409436 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=8-23=-15.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷3=-5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $4∤(-15).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $5∣(-15)$ because $(-15)÷5=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n-3≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $-18$\n- $-15$\n- $-9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97d3bcb5-c2f9-55ac-a2c8-208f62457770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409437 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-18)).$ Notice that $-3-(-18)=15,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $12∣(-3-(-15)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-3-(-15))÷12=12÷12=1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-9)).$ Notice that $-3-(-9)=6,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$-15$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $13≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a58d29a-b4cc-50fc-9d69-711422d71682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409438 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=13-(-5)=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $6∣18$ because $18÷6=3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n11≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $3$\n- $7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa9fc443-6ba1-5837-ae0a-5b71ce84408d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409439 #edb/ref 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $4,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $4$ numbers. \n\n![](images/06/0644cdd579eeaa9168808434167e1e007a698efeab2e5d031bf8b0159a245af4.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $4.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $4∤(11-1).$ Notice that $11-1=10,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-3).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-3)÷4=8÷4=2\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $4∣(11-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-7)÷4=4÷4=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$3$ and $7$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $12≡36\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $-9≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $21≡45\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae89ee49-3f2d-51e4-a2f6-ac3e918a674e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409440 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $2∣(12-36).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(12-36)÷2=-24÷2=-12\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $4∤(-9-18).$ Notice that $-9-18=-27,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $8\\,|\\,(21-45).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(21-45)÷8=-24÷8=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $-7≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $6≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b17c24c5-e750-5126-8785-6318018ca783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409441 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $-3≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=2$\n- $n=6$\n- $n=9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e77d765e-3b79-5534-bec0-79f592cc8d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-3-15=-18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷2=-9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷6=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-18)$ because $(-18)÷9=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n6≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $0$\n- $1$\n- $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff47ebb7-b97b-504b-85d7-8f24ec1bf6ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409443 #edb/ref 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $2,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $2$ numbers. \n\n![](images/93/93c588ff95bd8987e60ce2221ebef6e44c05039c13608ba35968a28c8582dd34.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $2.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-0)÷2=6÷2=3\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $2∤(6-1).$ Notice that $6-1=5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-2).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-2)÷2=4÷2=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$0$ and $2$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n7≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $2$\n- $10$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fffccd1b-296a-5eb0-9657-b20162f80bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409444 #edb/ref 1074 "To visualize congruence modulo $3,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $3$ numbers. \n\n![](images/46/46c64b99bf297e841b79bcc2e97c8a650b6ae94af8e11f2d4e85f2e8513def6c.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $3.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-1)÷3=6÷3=2\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $3∤(7-2).$ Notice that $7-2=5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-10).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-10)÷3=-3÷3=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$1$ and $10$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
